Below is a short mapping of the columns that will really matter for “toy‑sales‑by‑day” together with the typical conditions one would apply, followed by ten exploratory / step‑by‑step SQL statements (progressing from very simple look‑ups to the final aggregation that delivers the wanted sum of the four 5‑day symmetric moving averages).

Relevant columns and their potential usage
• product_category_name_translation.product_category_name_english : decide which English category (e.g. ‘toys’) we are interested in.  
• product_category_name_translation.product_category_name        : Portuguese “internal” category name that appears in the products table.  
• products.product_category_name                                 : joins the toy category to the individual products.  
• products.product_id                                            : joins products to order_items.  
• order_items.product_id                                         : links each order line to its product.  
• order_items.order_id                                           : links to the corresponding order (for the purchase date).  
• orders.order_purchase_timestamp                                : contains the calendar date we need for daily aggregation.  
Main filter conditions
• product_category_name_translation.product_category_name_english = 'toys'   – choose the “toy” rows.  
• DATE(orders.order_purchase_timestamp) BETWEEN '2017‑01‑01' AND '2018‑08‑29' – historical data for training the regression.  
• DATE(orders.order_purchase_timestamp) BETWEEN '2018‑12‑05' AND '2018‑12‑08' – the four forecast days we finally care about.  

Ten sample / instructional SQL queries

```sql
-- 1. Description: Which English category names contain the word “toy”?
SELECT DISTINCT pct."product_category_name_english"
FROM product_category_name_translation AS pct
WHERE pct."product_category_name_english" LIKE '%toy%' 
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 2. Description: Show the Portuguese names that translate to “toys”.
SELECT DISTINCT pct."product_category_name"
FROM product_category_name_translation AS pct
WHERE pct."product_category_name_english" = 'toys'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 3. Description: Verify that those Portuguese categories really exist in products.
SELECT DISTINCT p."product_category_name"
FROM products AS p
WHERE p."product_category_name" IN (
    SELECT pct."product_category_name"
    FROM product_category_name_translation AS pct
    WHERE pct."product_category_name_english" = 'toys'
)
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 4. Description: Pull one sample list of toy products (product_id ↔ Portuguese category).
SELECT DISTINCT p."product_id", p."product_category_name"
FROM products AS p
WHERE p."product_category_name" IN (
    SELECT pct."product_category_name"
    FROM product_category_name_translation AS pct
    WHERE pct."product_category_name_english" = 'toys'
)
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 5. Description: A first look at orders that contain *at least one* toy item.
SELECT DISTINCT oi."order_id"
FROM order_items AS oi
JOIN products    AS p  ON p."product_id" = oi."product_id"
WHERE p."product_category_name" IN (
    SELECT pct."product_category_name"
    FROM product_category_name_translation AS pct
    WHERE pct."product_category_name_english" = 'toys'
)
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 6. Description: Retrieve the purchase date together with each toy order line.
SELECT DATE(o."order_purchase_timestamp") AS sale_date,
       oi."order_id",
       oi."order_item_id"
FROM order_items AS oi
JOIN products AS p         ON p."product_id" = oi."product_id"
JOIN orders   AS o         ON o."order_id"  = oi."order_id"
WHERE p."product_category_name" IN (
    SELECT pct."product_category_name"
    FROM product_category_name_translation AS pct
    WHERE pct."product_category_name_english" = 'toys'
)
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 7. Description: Aggregate the **historical** daily toy‑sales count
--    that we will feed into the linear‑regression step (2017‑01‑01 – 2018‑08‑29).
SELECT DATE(o."order_purchase_timestamp")   AS sale_date,
       COUNT(*)                             AS qty_toy_sales
FROM   order_items AS oi
JOIN   products    AS p ON p."product_id" = oi."product_id"
JOIN   orders      AS o ON o."order_id"  = oi."order_id"
WHERE  p."product_category_name" IN (
          SELECT pct."product_category_name"
          FROM   product_category_name_translation AS pct
          WHERE  pct."product_category_name_english" = 'toys'
      )
  AND  DATE(o."order_purchase_timestamp") BETWEEN '2017-01-01' AND '2018-08-29'
GROUP  BY sale_date
ORDER  BY sale_date
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 8. Description: Compute slope & intercept of a simple linear regression
--    (#days since 2017‑01‑01 → qty_toy_sales).  SQLite can do it by
--    covariance / variance formula.
WITH daily AS (
    SELECT julianday(DATE(o."order_purchase_timestamp")) 
           - julianday('2017-01-01')           AS x,     -- independent variable
           COUNT(*)                           AS y      -- dependent variable
    FROM   order_items AS oi
    JOIN   products    AS p ON p."product_id" = oi."product_id"
    JOIN   orders      AS o ON o."order_id"  = oi."order_id"
    WHERE  p."product_category_name" IN (
              SELECT pct."product_category_name"
              FROM   product_category_name_translation AS pct
              WHERE  pct."product_category_name_english" = 'toys'
           )
      AND  DATE(o."order_purchase_timestamp") BETWEEN '2017-01-01' AND '2018-08-29'
    GROUP  BY DATE(o."order_purchase_timestamp")
)
SELECT 
    (COUNT(*) * SUM(x*y) - SUM(x) * SUM(y)) /
    (COUNT(*) * SUM(x*x) - SUM(x) * SUM(x))  AS slope,
    (SUM(y) - ( (COUNT(*) * SUM(x*y) - SUM(x) * SUM(y)) /
                (COUNT(*) * SUM(x*x) - SUM(x) * SUM(x)) ) * SUM(x)) / COUNT(*) AS intercept
FROM daily;
```

```sql
-- 9. Description: Use the regression parameters to **predict** toy sales
--    for 2018‑12‑05 … 2018‑12‑08, then build a 5‑day symmetric moving average
--    centred on each of those four days (two days before & after).
WITH
params AS (                 -- obtain slope & intercept once
    /* Copy‑paste the slope/intercept query (#8) or store its result */
    SELECT -1 AS dummy,      -- placeholder
           0  AS slope,
           0  AS intercept
),
forecast AS (               -- build the 4 prediction rows
    SELECT DATE(d)                             AS sale_date,
           (julianday(d) - julianday('2017-01-01'))  AS x
    FROM (VALUES
          ('2018-12-05'),
          ('2018-12-06'),
          ('2018-12-07'),
          ('2018-12-08')) v(d)
),
predicted AS (
    SELECT f.sale_date,
           p.slope * f.x + p.intercept  AS y_hat
    FROM   forecast AS f
    JOIN   params   AS p ON 1=1
),
win AS (                    -- attach the ±2 surrounding prediction rows
    SELECT p1.sale_date,
           AVG(p2.y_hat) AS mov_avg_5d
    FROM   predicted AS p1
    JOIN   predicted AS p2
           ON julianday(p2.sale_date) BETWEEN julianday(p1.sale_date,'-2 day')
                                          AND     julianday(p1.sale_date,'+2 day')
    GROUP  BY p1.sale_date
)
SELECT *
FROM win
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 10. Description: Finally sum the four 5‑day moving averages
WITH
params AS ( /* same as in query 9 */ SELECT 0 AS slope, 0 AS intercept ),
forecast AS (SELECT DATE(d) AS sale_date, julianday(d)-julianday('2017-01-01') AS x
             FROM (VALUES('2018-12-05'),('2018-12-06'),('2018-12-07'),('2018-12-08')) v(d)),
predicted AS (SELECT f.sale_date, p.slope*f.x + p.intercept AS y_hat
              FROM forecast f CROSS JOIN params p),
win AS (SELECT p1.sale_date,
               AVG(p2.y_hat) AS mov_avg_5d
        FROM   predicted p1
        JOIN   predicted p2
             ON julianday(p2.sale_date) BETWEEN julianday(p1.sale_date,'-2 day')
                                            AND julianday(p1.sale_date,'+2 day')
        GROUP  BY p1.sale_date)
SELECT SUM(mov_avg_5d) AS sum_of_four_moving_avgs
FROM   win;
```

Replace the slope & intercept placeholders in queries 9 and 10 with the numeric results you get from query 8; the last query will then return exactly the required “sum of the four 5‑day symmetric moving averages”.
